text:
marco: heads up — hitting an ESM/CJS import error when using the Redwood JS client inside Next.js middleware (edge runtime). stack: `ReferenceError: require is not defined`.
alyssa: ugh, same. Is the SDK publishing a CJS entry that gets pulled in by the middleware bundle?
jordan: middleware is strict ESM + no node builtins, so any transitive require or package.json "main" pointing to cjs will break. Next will try to bundle but sometimes pulls the wrong export.
emily: quick repro: in middleware.ts i did: `import redwood from '@redwood-inference/sdk'` and got the require error at runtime.
marco: i tried forcing ESM by importing `import * as redwood from '@redwood-inference/sdk/esm'` but that package doesn't exist upstream.
alyssa: options: (1) SDK provide an edge-compatible entry (exports.edge) (2) we add a small wrapper in our repo that only imports the ESM/edge friendly parts (3) use dynamic import in middleware? dynamic import might still pull cjs via the resolver.
jordan: FYI Next edge env disallows `fs`, `crypto.randomBytes`, sync crypto, and process.nextTick. so SDK must be pure web-friendly.
emily: i hacked a local fix by aliasing in next.config.js: 

```js
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  webpack: (config, { isServer }) => {
    if (process.env.USE_EDGE_PKG) {
      config.resolve.alias['@redwood-inference/sdk'] = require.resolve('./src/edge-client-proxy.js');
    }
    return config;
  },
};
```

marco: what did you put in edge-client-proxy.js?
emily: super tiny wrapper that only uses fetch + web crypto and re-exports the minimal call we need: 

```js
// edge-client-proxy.js
export async function generate(opts) {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_KEY}` },
    body: JSON.stringify(opts),
  });
  return res.json();
}
export default { generate }
```

alyssa: neat. For types: TypeScript complained because the wrapper lacked typings. I added a small `.d.ts` with the exported function signature.
jordan: ideally SDK ships an "edge" export target in package.json exports: 

```json
"exports": {
  ".": { "import": "./dist/index.mjs", "edge": "./dist/index.edge.mjs", "default": "./dist/index.cjs" }
}
```

marco: +1. Also request SDK team to strip Node-only deps (debug, some perf libs) behind conditional exports so bundlers don't grab them for edge builds.
emily: can CI catch regressions? our ci-bot: triggered a tiny workflow.
ci-bot: Build matrix includes a Next edge smoke test now (middleware + Vercel edge runtime simulator). Results: failing on 2.1.x of SDK because of `require` in logger shim.
alyssa: filed a small issue in the SDK repo with repro and suggested export change. link: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/412
marco: for short term, recommend teams add the alias workaround + add a tiny typed wrapper in monorepo. I'll post a short recipe in devex with code snippets and ci step.
emily: thanks — will test the alias approach on the payment webhook middleware tomorrow.
jordan: long term: SDK should publish an edge bundle, add types for it, and CI should run an edge-targeted bundle resolution test.
marco: ok closing the thread when the SDK issue lands. :thumbsup:
